  "worked_solution": "The area of the region bounded by the curve $y=6{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2}),$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}6{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))={sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}6{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{x}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{(x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3[(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}))-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))] \\\\  & =3[(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-1)-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1)] \\\\  & =3(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
